#: folio/dpi-h-ra/appendix/p-c-business-services-components-benefits-package-registry-37d21e.md:1
msgid "Progress toward universal health coverage depends on knowing, with precision and consistency, what services people are entitled to receive. In most countries today, that knowledge is fragmented — held in separate documents, scheme-specific databases, donor agreements, and operational systems that do not share a common reference. Some challenges faced in countries are that a provider is unable to reliably determine at the point of care what a benefit package covers and a Ministry of Health cannot assess coverage gaps or identify which populations are unprotected without manually reconciling information held across multiple institutions. The result is a system that formally promises coverage but cannot operationally deliver it with consistency or transparency. At the same time, authoritative benefit definitions are only one input to financing decisions: where claims adjudication, reimbursement, or other consequential financing actions depend on clinical or administrative evidence of service delivery, medical necessity, or fulfilment of programme conditions, confirmation of package coverage, person-specific eligibility, provider credential or empanelment status, and technical transaction validity does not by itself establish that all substantive requirements for payment have been met. Countries should therefore define clearly within their financing architecture which operational systems and governance roles bear responsibility for assessing the sufficiency, clinical appropriateness, and provenance of that evidence."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/appendix/t-f-79f600.md:11
