#: folio/dpi-h-ra/appendix/p-e-deab37.md:7
msgid "Shared DPI-H reference components versus operational financing systems. Within the scope of DPI-H, the principal domain-specific shared reference component is the Benefits Package Registry, which publishes authoritative benefit package definitions, coverage parameters, and testable machine-readable reference rules. It does not determine person-specific eligibility, enrol beneficiaries, manage provider contracts, or adjudicate claims; those functions sit in operational systems outside DPI-H — Beneficiary Registries and enrolment platforms, Contract and Benefits Management Systems (CBMS), provider contracting and tariff systems, prior-authorisation and claims adjudication engines, and public financial management (PFM) systems — which consume the shared registries."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/appendix/p-e-b1992f.md:3
msgid "Shared DPI-H reference components versus operational workforce systems: the mapping centres on three shared DPI-H components — the Health Workforce Registry (a core foundational registry reused across clinical care, surveillance, supply chain, and health financing), the Health Facility Registry, and the Terminology Service — resting on foundational-DPI identity, trust/verifiable credentials, payment rails, data exchange, and single sign-on services. However, these shared registries alone cannot deliver Goal 2 without the operational systems that consume and update them: Human Resources for Health Information Systems (HRHIS) and employer HRIS, civil-service and facility payroll, contract management, public financial management (PFM), time and attendance, shift scheduling, deployment management, professional regulatory council platforms, clinical privileging and supervision tools, pre-service education and accreditation systems, learning-management systems (LMS), and workforce-planning and health labour market modelling applications. In particular, foundational payment DPI provides disbursement rails but does not determine employment validity, salary calculation, attendance, deductions, payment authorisation, or budget availability (which belong to HR, contract, attendance, payroll, and PFM systems); similarly, the Health Workforce Registry holds authoritative qualification and high-level continuing professional development (CPD) compliance references, whereas detailed learning activities, curricula, supervision logs, and competency assessments belong to LMS, education, and accreditation systems."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/appendix/t-c-metadata-registries-and-foundati-client-registry-a1d132.md:31
msgid "Shared Health Record ID synchronisation. The Client Registry shall support synchronisation of person identifiers with the Lifelong Health Record (or Shared Health Record), ensuring that the canonical HUID is consistently used as the person anchor across clinical and identity systems. (OpenHIE CRF-4)"
msgstr ""

